1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,619
A gas stove flame.

2
00:00:01,619 --> 00:00:02,696
A burning candle.

3
00:00:02,696 --> 00:00:03,823
A tank of petrol.

4
00:00:03,823 --> 00:00:06,422
All three run on the
same family of molecules.

5
00:00:06,822 --> 00:00:10,031
They are alkanes, chains of
only carbon and hydrogen.

6
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:13,648
Yet one is a gas, one is a
liquid, and one is a solid.

7
00:00:14,098 --> 00:00:16,019
Diya sees it in the kitchen.

8
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:20,703
The stove gas floats away into the air,
but the candle wax just sits there,

9
00:00:20,703 --> 00:00:21,603
solid.

10
00:00:21,636 --> 00:00:23,909
Both are made of carbon and hydrogen.

11
00:00:23,909 --> 00:00:25,768
So why are they so different?

12
00:00:26,268 --> 00:00:29,954
By the end, you will know how
alkanes behave, and how they react.

13
00:00:30,604 --> 00:00:31,795
So what decides it?

14
00:00:31,795 --> 00:00:33,193
Think of cooked noodles.

15
00:00:33,543 --> 00:00:35,611
Short noodles slip apart easily.

16
00:00:35,611 --> 00:00:38,074
Long noodles tangle and stick together.

17
00:00:38,424 --> 00:00:40,139
Alkane molecules are like that.

18
00:00:40,139 --> 00:00:43,887
They are non polar, so they hold
each other only by weak pulls, called

19
00:00:43,887 --> 00:00:45,145
van der Waals forces.

20
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:49,927
A longer chain has more surface to
touch its neighbours, so the pulls add

21
00:00:49,927 --> 00:00:50,827
up.

22
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:54,049
More pull means more heat is
needed to pull them apart.

23
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:55,897
So the boiling point goes up.

24
00:00:56,297 --> 00:01:00,516
That is why alkanes with one to four
carbons are gases, five to seventeen

25
00:01:00,516 --> 00:01:04,525
are liquids, and eighteen or more
are solids, at room temperature.

26
00:01:05,175 --> 00:01:07,849
Now watch branching on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:08,199 --> 00:01:12,005
Here are three molecules with the
same formula, C five H twelve.

28
00:01:12,405 --> 00:01:14,295
Pentane is one straight chain.

29
00:01:14,295 --> 00:01:17,477
It boils at three hundred
and nine point one kelvin.

30
00:01:17,877 --> 00:01:21,645
Move one carbon to the side,
and we get two methyl butane.

31
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:24,909
It boils lower, at three
hundred point nine kelvin.

32
00:01:25,309 --> 00:01:29,520
Move two carbons, and we get two
two dimethyl propane, almost a ball.

33
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:33,182
It boils at only two hundred and
eighty two point five kelvin.

34
00:01:33,582 --> 00:01:34,482
Why?

35
00:01:34,012 --> 00:01:37,526
A ball touches its neighbours at
fewer points than a long chain.

36
00:01:37,926 --> 00:01:40,793
Less contact, weaker
pulls, lower boiling point.

37
00:01:40,793 --> 00:01:42,861
Same mass, but the shape decides.

38
00:01:43,511 --> 00:01:44,971
One more physical fact.

39
00:01:44,971 --> 00:01:46,848
Alkanes do not mix with water.

40
00:01:47,248 --> 00:01:49,504
Water is polar, and alkanes are non polar.

41
00:01:49,504 --> 00:01:51,798
Like dissolves like,
so the two stay apart.

42
00:01:52,198 --> 00:01:56,652
Now compare hexane, a straight chain
of six carbons, with two two dimethyl

43
00:01:56,652 --> 00:01:59,401
butane, also six carbons, but bunched up.

44
00:01:59,801 --> 00:02:00,811
Pause and think.

45
00:02:00,811 --> 00:02:03,003
Which one boils at a higher temperature?

46
00:02:06,203 --> 00:02:07,103
Hexane!

47
00:02:06,825 --> 00:02:09,238
Its long chain touches more neighbours.

48
00:02:09,238 --> 00:02:12,414
More contact, stronger
pull, higher boiling point.

49
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:14,166
Now the chemistry.

50
00:02:14,166 --> 00:02:15,227
Alkanes are lazy.

51
00:02:15,227 --> 00:02:18,027
But in light, chlorine can
replace their hydrogens.

52
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:19,253
This is halogenation.

53
00:02:19,253 --> 00:02:21,043
Let's follow it, link by link.

54
00:02:21,393 --> 00:02:23,007
Link one, initiation.

55
00:02:23,007 --> 00:02:25,672
Light splits a chlorine molecule evenly.

56
00:02:25,672 --> 00:02:28,236
Each chlorine atom keeps one electron.

57
00:02:28,236 --> 00:02:32,230
An atom with one unpaired
electron is called a free radical.

58
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:33,880
Link two.

59
00:02:33,779 --> 00:02:36,830
A chlorine radical pulls
a hydrogen off methane.

60
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:40,826
It makes hydrogen chloride, and
leaves a methyl radical behind.

61
00:02:41,576 --> 00:02:42,476
Link three.

62
00:02:42,415 --> 00:02:46,346
The methyl radical grabs one chlorine
atom from a chlorine molecule.

63
00:02:46,346 --> 00:02:49,949
Chloromethane forms, and a new
chlorine radical is set free.

64
00:02:50,699 --> 00:02:53,199
That new radical starts link two again.

65
00:02:53,199 --> 00:02:55,557
One spark keeps a whole chain going.

66
00:02:55,557 --> 00:02:58,248
Links two and three
are called propagation.

67
00:02:58,998 --> 00:03:00,305
Link four, termination.

68
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:02,499
Two radicals meet and
pair their electrons.

69
00:03:02,499 --> 00:03:04,616
Two methyl radicals can even make ethane.

70
00:03:04,616 --> 00:03:05,573
The chain stops.

71
00:03:06,323 --> 00:03:07,223
Remember this.

72
00:03:07,218 --> 00:03:10,791
Alkanes are lazy, but light,
heat or a catalyst wakes them up.

73
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:13,170
Now, one worked example.

74
00:03:13,170 --> 00:03:16,798
How much heat do we get by
burning eight grams of methane?

75
00:03:17,098 --> 00:03:17,998
Step one.

76
00:03:17,955 --> 00:03:19,928
Write the balanced equation.

77
00:03:19,928 --> 00:03:23,948
Methane plus two oxygen gives
carbon dioxide plus two water.

78
00:03:24,698 --> 00:03:25,598
Step two.

79
00:03:25,388 --> 00:03:29,313
Burning one mole of methane gives out
eight hundred and ninety kilojoules.

80
00:03:29,313 --> 00:03:31,213
The minus sign means heat leaves.

81
00:03:31,963 --> 00:03:32,863
Step three.

82
00:03:32,844 --> 00:03:35,603
The molar mass of
methane is sixteen grams.

83
00:03:35,603 --> 00:03:37,681
So eight grams is half a mole.

84
00:03:38,431 --> 00:03:39,331
Step four.

85
00:03:39,122 --> 00:03:42,716
Half of eight hundred and ninety is
four hundred and forty five kilojoules

86
00:03:42,716 --> 00:03:43,616
of heat.

87
00:03:44,076 --> 00:03:45,971
This is why alkanes make such good fuels.

88
00:03:46,621 --> 00:03:48,388
Alkanes have more tricks.

89
00:03:48,388 --> 00:03:52,878
With a catalyst and a limited supply
of air, burning stops halfway.

90
00:03:52,878 --> 00:03:54,848
This is controlled oxidation.

91
00:03:54,848 --> 00:03:59,653
Methane over copper, at five hundred
and twenty three kelvin and one hundred

92
00:03:59,653 --> 00:04:01,613
atmospheres, gives methanol.

93
00:04:02,013 --> 00:04:02,913
Isomerisation.

94
00:04:02,816 --> 00:04:06,307
Hexane, heated with aluminium chloride
and hydrogen chloride, rearranges

95
00:04:06,307 --> 00:04:07,483
into branched chains.

96
00:04:07,883 --> 00:04:08,783
Aromatisation.

97
00:04:08,764 --> 00:04:12,706
Hexane, at seven hundred and seventy
three kelvin, over metal oxides on

98
00:04:12,706 --> 00:04:14,894
alumina, closes into a benzene ring.

99
00:04:15,294 --> 00:04:20,172
Methane with steam over nickel, at
twelve hundred and seventy three kelvin,

100
00:04:20,172 --> 00:04:22,548
gives carbon monoxide and hydrogen.

101
00:04:22,948 --> 00:04:23,848
And pyrolysis.

102
00:04:23,813 --> 00:04:27,084
Strong heat cracks big alkanes
into smaller alkanes and alkenes.

103
00:04:27,084 --> 00:04:29,248
Oil gas is made from kerosene this way.

104
00:04:29,898 --> 00:04:31,360
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:04:31,710 --> 00:04:34,448
Students think the chlorination of
methane gives only chloromethane.

106
00:04:34,848 --> 00:04:35,748
Wrong.

107
00:04:35,311 --> 00:04:36,520
The chain keeps going.

108
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:39,827
With more chlorine, we also get
dichloromethane, chloroform and carbon

109
00:04:39,827 --> 00:04:40,727
tetrachloride.

110
00:04:40,595 --> 00:04:41,495
A mixture.

111
00:04:41,906 --> 00:04:43,202
Where is this asked?

112
00:04:43,202 --> 00:04:45,525
Board papers ask the free radical steps.

113
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:49,545
Entrance tests ask the boiling point
order and the products of reactions.

114
00:04:50,145 --> 00:04:51,335
Exam level now.

115
00:04:51,335 --> 00:04:55,844
In the chlorination of methane in
light, which step can form ethane?

116
00:04:55,844 --> 00:04:57,085
Pause and solve.

117
00:05:02,485 --> 00:05:03,972
The answer is D.

118
00:05:03,972 --> 00:05:05,021
Termination.

119
00:05:05,521 --> 00:05:08,779
Initiation only splits chlorine
into two chlorine radicals.

120
00:05:09,279 --> 00:05:13,219
The two propagation steps make hydrogen
chloride and chloromethane, and

121
00:05:13,219 --> 00:05:15,098
always leave a radical behind.

122
00:05:15,598 --> 00:05:18,421
Only in termination do
two methyl radicals meet.

123
00:05:18,421 --> 00:05:21,478
They pair their electrons
into a carbon carbon bond.

124
00:05:21,478 --> 00:05:22,542
That is ethane.

125
00:05:23,142 --> 00:05:24,683
Here is your recap.

126
00:05:24,683 --> 00:05:26,267
Screenshot this card.

127
00:05:29,467 --> 00:05:31,653
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

128
00:05:32,053 --> 00:05:32,953
One.

129
00:05:32,469 --> 00:05:35,569
Why does pentane boil higher
than two two dimethyl propane?

130
00:05:36,369 --> 00:05:37,269
Two.

131
00:05:36,789 --> 00:05:39,673
What does light do in the
first step of halogenation?

132
00:05:40,473 --> 00:05:41,373
Three.

133
00:05:40,962 --> 00:05:43,899
How much heat does one mole
of methane give on burning?

134
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:48,959
Next time: can a molecule change its
shape without breaking a single bond?

135
00:05:48,959 --> 00:05:51,215
We find out in conformations of ethane.
